  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Conduct annual audits by an IRDAI-approved auditor, ensuring the accuracy and completeness of financial disclosures. The audit should cover financial statements, internal controls, and compliance with regulatory standards.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:5b85ee29d373">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Risk Management and Compliance for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10">Risk Management &amp; Compliance Manual</data>
  <data key="d11">Controls: A range of internal controls and procedures are implemented to mitigate operational risks and ensure process efficiency. These controls include automated controls, segregation of duties, and regular audits, supporting SecureLife's commitment to effective risk management.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:af2d953a883d">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Regular updates to the Board and management should be provided to ensure they are informed of regulatory developments and compliance requirements.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:338ac892379e">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The Board should ensure that the company has a sound financial management system in place, including accurate record-keeping and timely financial reporting.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:4b743ed25db3">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">A strong relationship with the Board and management is essential for the Audit Committee to fulfill its oversight responsibilities effectively.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:252f9e4cd573">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Risk Management and Compliance for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10">Risk Management &amp; Compliance Manual</data>
  <data key="d11">Documentation: All risk identification and assessment activities are documented, including the tools used, findings, and actions taken. This documentation promotes transparency and accountability, supporting the company's commitment to effective risk management.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:1603d3454121">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">A comprehensive risk management framework should be in place, incorporating policies, procedures, and tools for effective risk management.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:f01641cfa138">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Establish a system for ongoing risk monitoring and assessment, ensuring that risk exposures are identified and managed promptly. Regular reporting to the Board on significant risk issues and mitigation measures is essential for effective governance.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:b4333366c11a">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The Audit Committee should review and approve the company’s financial statements, ensuring accuracy and compliance with accounting standards.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:edbf99a662b7">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Composed of at least three members, with a majority being Independent Directors to ensure objectivity and impartiality. The committee’s role is to oversee financial reporting, internal controls, and liaising with internal and external auditors.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:b7c3354863e5">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The compliance function should ensure that audit reports are communicated to the Board and management, supporting informed decision-making and strategic planning.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:88b70dc4bbd3">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The Board should comprise a diverse mix of Executive, Non-Executive, and Independent Directors, with a minimum of one-third being Independent Directors to ensure impartiality. This diverse composition allows for a wider range of perspectives and expertise, which is crucial for balanced decision-maki</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:196456be2316">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">A diverse composition ensures that the committee benefits from a wide range of expertise and experience in risk management.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:9eb3249e2784">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Directors are typically appointed for terms not exceeding five years per term, with provisions for reappointment, ensuring continuity and the infusion of new ideas. This balance helps maintain a dynamic and effective Board.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:27abc75ed61e">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The staggered board approach, where the terms of Directors are spread out, ensures that only a portion of the Board is up for reappointment at any given time, providing stability.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:0c7f379d0ed8">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The Board should meet regularly, at least quarterly, to discuss strategic initiatives, financial performance, and regulatory compliance, ensuring active governance.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:2939f5f302a0">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">A strong relationship with the Board and management is essential for the CCO to fulfill their oversight responsibilities effectively.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:0dcdc8c4e4cc">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Collaboration with internal and external auditors may be necessary to gain insights into risk management practices and identify areas for improvement.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:f6a96f63744e">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The compliance function should work closely with internal and external auditors to address any issues or concerns identified during the audit process.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:5066eab8ee5a">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The internal audit function should work closely with the compliance function and other departments to address audit findings and implement improvements.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:e2d300c1d15b">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">A strong relationship with auditors is essential for the compliance function to fulfill its oversight responsibilities effectively.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:d495ebfa6aac">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Regular meetings with internal and external auditors should be held to discuss audit findings and address any issues or concerns.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:45443ad63284">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Regular meetings should be held to review risk assessments, discuss emerging risks, and evaluate the effectiveness of risk mitigation strategies.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:5221c48e3d45">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Regular reviews of audit findings and recommendations should be conducted to identify areas for improvement and address any gaps or weaknesses.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:ea5c8ca1be5b">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">The internal audit function should establish a risk-based audit plan that focuses on areas of greatest risk and potential impact.</data>
</node>
<node id="chunk:2770dd993ac3">
  <data key="d0">policy_chunk</data>
  <data key="d9">Governance and Corporate Structure for an Insurance Company.docx</data>
  <data key="d10"></data>
  <data key="d11">Regular audits and assessments should be conducted to evaluate the effectiveness of compliance policies and identify areas for improvement.</data>
</node>
